>
>This is fine as long as no one in the group has studied these
>languages.  As a doctoral candidate in Classics (Greek, Latin)
>I find that this is, however, very disappointing for me in any RPG.  
>Even when it's in a language I don't know, I find that instead of
>inspiring me to think of "magical lore" or "mystical incantation"
>I think of how the game designer is trying to build his fantasy
>world with things that are *to him* other-worldly, where in fact
>they are "this-worldly."

Yes.  Good point.
Perhaps if we had a good other-worldly language.
Let's see, a game which has hobbits, trolls, orcs, elves, dwarves, wizards...
 							      ^[sic]
Hmmmm.
Can anyone think of a language that would fit such a world?
This game is so original.  It has stolen nothing from the real world
(soldiers, tourists, killer bees, dogs, jackals, rats)  or
from the real world's mythology (Keystone Kops, demons, Valkyries, nymphs).

If we could just think of a magical language that would sound realistic
in the mouths of elves...
But who would sit around and make up such a thing.  It would
take a lifetime.  Plus, for any language, you must have a history and
mythology upon which to build it.  Why it would be material enough
for many books.  Perhaps even the greatest Fantasy trilogy of all time.

Not likely buddy.  This is the 20th century!  We don't have time
to sit around and think 'On Fairy Stories' for the rest of our lives.
Plus, it would take a great deal of intelligence.  You'd have to get
a real linguist for it to be good.  People like that are usually too
busy teaching at great universities like Oxford for them to have time
making up languages.


